  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a stated choice experiment using street-level images rather than observed GPS tracks of actual route choices.",
      "what": "Using a stated choice experiment and a street-level image dataset, this study extracted safety perception scores from cycling-perspective images using a computer vision model. Mixed logit models revealed that cyclists are willing to accept 64 additional seconds of travel time for a one-unit increase in perceived safety on a scale from -2 to +2. The analysis shows that older cyclists, recreational riders, and those with positive cycling attitudes prioritize safety, whereas commuters prioritize speed.",
      "why": "It quantifies the trade-off between travel time and perceived safety in monetary or time-equivalent terms, allowing planners to incorporate subjective safety improvements directly into cost-benefit analyses and targeted infrastructure upgrades."
    }
  },
